    "relation": "cites_only",
    "tranche": "T6",
    "tranche_reason": "The paper tests sequential visual-storytelling jailbreaks in multimodal models, not fine-tuning safety loss, response-position KL, compliant prefixes, suffixes or decoding changes, parameter localization, causes of shallowness, or safety deepening.",
    "gate_note": "The reported attack-success rates measure harmful compliance elicited by comic sequences, a related but different mechanism from the claim versions. The paper also reports comparisons, component ablations, and defense results, but these evaluate SCJ and its defenses rather than any listed version; they are not included as claim-version results. Appendix prompts direct the target model, not an AI reading or reviewing the paper.",
    "test_reason": "The paper proposes and evaluates a multimodal comic-sequence jailbreak (SCJ). It does not fine-tune models, so V1 and V2 are not tested. It measures no per-position KL (V3), and does not localize parameters (V5). It neither analyses the causes of shallowness (V6) nor tests deepening (V7). It also does not isolate prefill, suffix or decoding manipulations (V4). Its only link to the original sources is citing Qi et al. (arXiv:2310.03693) for the GPT-4 judge scoring scheme. The reader's 'discusses' overstates the link; 'cites_only' is more accurate.",
    "authors_conclusion": "The record's paraphrase is accurate. The authors conclude that SCJ achieves about 83.5% average ASR, roughly 46% above prior visual jailbreaks. Procedural categories are most exposed, and Llama Guard and LLaVA Guard give only partial mitigation. They call for narrative-aware multimodal safety mechanisms.",
    "recipe": "different",
    "recipe_differences": "The study proposes a multimodal comic-sequence jailbreak, not fine-tuning, token-position measurement, or a test of the original studies' methods.",
    "method_sensitivity": "Reported SCJ success varies substantially by model and dataset. The text also describes category and prompt/format differences in its own SCJ ablations, but those are not tests of the listed claim versions.",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "SCJ comic-sequence visual attacks reach high harmful-output ASR across eleven MLLMs on MM-SafetyBench (mean 83.39%) and HADES (mean 83.58%). The lowest values are for GPT-5 (47.25% / 22.53%) and Claude-3.5 (50.33% / 57.07%).",
        "proposed_claim": "Distributing harmful intent across sequential comic panels bypasses MLLM safety alignment far more often than direct text or single-image attacks.",
