    "problems": "No uncertainty estimates are stated for the attack success rates. GPT-4 is used as the harmfulness judge, and each query is tried up to five times with success defined by the maximum score. The paper notes that OpenAI models were accessed through Azure, which adds a moderation layer.",
    "rows_left_out": "Left out the visual-format and prompt-template ablation numbers in Sections V.E and Figures 4–5, prior-method comparisons in Tables I–II, and defense-condition results in Section V.F and Tables III–IV. These evaluate SCJ, its component choices, comparators, or defenses rather than a listed claim version.",
    "untested": [
      {
        "version": "V1",
        "parts": "No harmful fine-tuning is performed."
      },
      {
        "version": "V2",
        "parts": "No benign fine-tuning is performed."
      },
      {
        "version": "V3",
        "parts": "No token-position KL or response-position analysis."
      },
      {
        "version": "V4",
        "parts": "No prefilled compliant openings, adversarial suffixes or decoding changes are tested in isolation. PT2 tells the model to start its output with '1.' and forbids apologies, but the ablation mixes this with list framing and comic inputs, so no clean opening-bypass effect can be read."
      },
      {
        "version": "V5",
        "parts": "No parameter or rank localization."
      },
      {
        "version": "V6",
        "parts": "No analysis of why alignment is shallow."
      },
      {
        "version": "V7",
        "parts": "No deepening or augmented safety training is evaluated."
      }
    ],
    "missing": [],
    "verdict_notes": "Rows correctly carry versions ['none']. The rows list baseline as 'not stated', but Tables I–II report a direct-text baseline per model; for example, GPT-4o has 2.63% on MM-SafetyBench and 0.67% on HADES. These could fill the baseline field if the rows are reused for a fits_no_version claim. Table I's Avg column for SCJ (90.97%) does not match the mean of the per-model values (83.39%, as stated in Section V.A). Table II's 83.72% is likewise inconsistent with the text's 83.58%. ASR uses best-of-five attempts with a GPT-4 judge, which inflates success relative to single-sample metrics. OpenAI models were accessed via Azure, which adds a moderation layer."
  },
